when we think of the risk of concussions  in athletes sports like football and  hockey often come to mind but  researchers have also been focusing on a  different sport how about this soccer  mild but repetitive hits to the head not  necessarily concussions can be risked  for the brain injury known as cte our  chief medical correspondent that's dr  john lapook has been looking into the  growing concerns about cte and soccer a  game played by an estimated 13 million  american children including vlad's  nephew we just learned first on cbs  mornings john interviewed retired u.s  soccer star bruce murray about his  growing fears for his health john good  morning most people don't think about  sarcasm no that's true good morning gail  cte is a stealth disease there's no cure  and it can only be diagnosed after death  symptoms include trouble with memory  problems organizing and planning simple  activities and changes in behavior such  as lack of motivation or having a short  temper today we're showing you the  uncertainty for a former elite player  who's now experiencing changes in his  memory  swinging  in 1990 crews marry the scorer bruce  murray was at the top of his game murray  with a header yes murray was a star at  clemson it was time for the bruce murray  show again before playing forward for  team usa it's the greatest feeling to be  able to travel the world and do what we  did murray nice run by bruce as he moved  into professional soccer murray  continued his reputation for being  fearless his aggressive play sometimes  left him seeing stars  how did those concussions generally  happen i was playing for the us and  saudi arabia and one of the saudi  players came right through and his knee  caught me in the head i was on my hands  and knees trying to find where my  equilibrium was but for the next six  weeks to a month i would go places and  not know why i was there back then they  would just say you got your bell rung  you heard that expression oh yeah yeah  get back out there or be tough murray  stayed tough through multiple  concussions and collisions he retired in  1995 at age 29. what made you finally  hang up your sneakers i was suffering  from post-concussion lack of sleep and i  have a hard time with light headaches my  body just felt like beaten up where do  you want to go for four years right when  we visited murray and his family in  maryland two years ago he was troubled  by recent lapses in memory so how's your  airplane coming there buddy including  one that left him especially shaken my  wife and my son and i were at the store  and i was in the car and they came out  and they came to the car and i said i  said oh sorry ma'am  do you need to get by and then  she kind of like looked at me like what  were you are you okay and then i was  like oh oh wait a second that's you  that you was the woman he had married  eight years earlier lynn murray when  bruce looked at you and didn't recognize  you  what was that like for you  it was shocking  it told me a lot  you know  i'm realizing now  that we don't have  the future that we were hoping for  so  it's the uncertainty  yeah good job kelly because of bruce's  history of concussions the murrays  reached out to boston university's  center for chronic traumatic  encephalopathy or cte the issue is the  repetitive hits that the players take dr  anne mckee is the center's director and  believes concussions are not the only  cause of cte i think there's been such  an emphasis on concussion in these  contact sports it's the hits that are  asymptomatic that the player plays right  through and over time those injuries  accumulate and lead to a high risk for  cte murray recalls heading the ball up  to 20 times a day during practice drills  but the balls were coming in at full  pace and you're just trying to get a  piece of it and get it in the net and  with your head oh yeah and many times  you would stagger back to the line and  your teammate would be like man that  looked terrible you okay you're like nah  yeah i'm fine i'm fine back then nobody  was saying hey you know maybe this is a  problem nobody said that yeah since cte  can only be diagnosed after death the bu  researchers scanned murray's head for  clues and they found one  his brain tissue seen here in gray had  atrophied or shrunk  compare that with this normal scan where  the gray brain tissue fills much of the  skull  do you think that heading should be  allowed in soccer there's an argument  for taking heading out of soccer those  balls are coming from a great distance  at a tremendous velocity and that  impacts the head and leads to these  injuries later on u.s soccer which  governs the sport from kindergarten  through the elite level has extensive  concussion protocols  and it bans or limits headers in  children 13 and younger but when it  comes to sub-concussive hits in older  players u.s soccer told cbs news it is  awaiting more research  the organization declined our request  for an on-camera interview think about  some of the military cases dr mckee has  examined the brains of hundreds of  deceased veterans and athletes including  football player aaron hernandez who died  by suicide while serving a life sentence  for murder  she thinks more needs to be done to  protect athletes of all ages is the  damage that you see in football similar  to the damage you've been seeing with  soccer yeah the changes look identical  whether they played rugby ice hockey  football or soccer it's the same  repetitive hits to the head we meet  again when we checked with bruce and  lynn murray recently they told us his  condition appears to be stable but he  still struggles with memory focus and  balance good go with pace with pace  excellent excellent good work good work  he still enjoys coaching youth sucker  good come find me come on come on come  find me there you go but believes the  sport he loves may need changes to make  it safer for everyone i think u.s soccer  needs to address 